## Proof

We work formally, treating the Fourier transform as

$$\hat{f}(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)\,e^{-i\omega x}\,dx,$$

and we freely interchange the order of integration (justified, e.g., by Fubini's theorem under suitable integrability assumptions on $f$ and $g$, which we regard as part of the formal setting).

---

### Part 1. $\hat{h}(\omega)=\hat{f}(\omega)\hat{g}(\omega)$

By definition of the Fourier transform and of $h$,

$$
\hat{h}(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} h(x)\,e^{-i\omega x}\,dx
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\!\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,g(\xi)\,d\xi\right] e^{-i\omega x}\,dx.
$$

Interchange the order of integration (Fubini):

$$
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\!\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,g(\xi)\,e^{-i\omega x}\,dx\,d\xi.
$$

Factor out the factor depending only on $\xi$:

$$
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(\xi)\!\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,e^{-i\omega x}\,dx\right] d\xi.
$$

In the inner integral perform the change of variable $u=x-\xi$, so $x=u+\xi$, $dx=du$:

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,e^{-i\omega x}\,dx
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(u)\,e^{-i\omega(u+\xi)}\,du
=e^{-i\omega\xi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(u)\,e^{-i\omega u}\,du
=e^{-i\omega\xi}\,\hat{f}(\omega).
$$

Substituting back,

$$
\hat{h}(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(\xi)\,e^{-i\omega\xi}\,\hat{f}(\omega)\,d\xi
=\hat{f}(\omega)\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(\xi)\,e^{-i\omega\xi}\,d\xi
=\hat{f}(\omega)\,\hat{g}(\omega).
$$

Hence

$$\boxed{\;\hat{h}(\omega)=\hat{f}(\omega)\,\hat{g}(\omega)\;}$$

---

### Part 2. Commutativity of convolution

We must show

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,g(\xi)\,d\xi
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(x-\xi)\,f(\xi)\,d\xi.
$$

Starting from the left-hand side, perform the change of variable $\eta=x-\xi$, so that $\xi=x-\eta$ and $d\xi=-d\eta$. As $\xi\to-\infty$, $\eta\to+\infty$, and as $\xi\to+\infty$, $\eta\to-\infty$; the reversal of limits absorbs the minus sign:

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,g(\xi)\,d\xi
=\int_{+\infty}^{-\infty} f(\eta)\,g(x-\eta)\,(-d\eta)
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(\eta)\,g(x-\eta)\,d\eta.
$$

Renaming the dummy variable $\eta\to\xi$,

$$
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(x-\xi)\,f(\xi)\,d\xi,
$$

which is precisely the right-hand side. Therefore

$$\boxed{\;\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x-\xi)\,g(\xi)\,d\xi=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(x-\xi)\,f(\xi)\,d\xi\;}$$

---

Both identities are established by purely formal manipulation: a single interchange of integration order (Part 1) and a single linear change of variable (Part 2), together with the observation that the Fourier kernel $e^{-i\omega x}$ factorizes as $e^{-i\omega u}e^{-i\omega\xi}$ under the shift $x=u+\xi$.

